The patterns, verified:

```js
// Object — by key, rename, default, rest
const { a, b: renamed, c = 5, ...restObj } = { a: 1, b: 2, x: 9, y: 10 };
// a=1, renamed=2, c=5 (default — key absent), restObj={x:9,y:10} ✓

// Array — by position, skip, rest
const [first, , third, ...restArr] = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];
// first=10, third=30 (20 SKIPPED), restArr=[40,50] ✓

// Swap without a temp — the classic
let p = 1, q = 2; [p, q] = [q, p];   // p=2, q=1 ✓

// Nested — drill into structure
const { name, address: { city } } = { name: 'Al', address: { city: 'Blr' } };
// name='Al', city='Blr' ✓

// Function params — the options-object idiom (production pattern)
function draw({ size = 10, color = 'red' } = {}) { return `${color} ${size}`; }
draw();            // 'red 10' — both defaults fire ✓
draw({ size: 20 }); // 'red 20' — only size overrides ✓
```

Why it matters (the interview core):
- Named parameters from an options object: callers see WHAT each argument is (`draw({color, size})` beats `draw(color, size)`), order-independent, extensible without breaking call sites
- Selective extraction: pull 2 fields from a 20-field API response in one line
- React/hooks idiom: `const { useState, useEffect } = React;` — the ecosystem runs on destructuring

The failure modes worth naming: destructuring a property that's undefined THROWS (`const { a: { b } } = {}` → TypeError: cannot destructure undefined) — guard with defaults at each level (`= {}`); and array destructuring an object / object destructuring an array silently misbehaves rather than erroring.
